derobertis/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nickderobertis/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nickderobertis/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nickderobertis/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nickderobertis/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nickderobertis/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nickderobertis/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"## Result: the bar as ruled is met on head `911aba50`, run [41183966027](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41183966027)\n\n**The user's ruling of 2026-10-31:** this change may land with test-windows slightly over 1,620 s, within its 30-minute timeout, because that job's time varies from run to run on the same code (21.8 to 29.3 minutes this round). lint, test-linux and test-macos must stay at or under 1,620 s on the qualifying run.\n\nOn run 41183966027 of the head now in review, `911aba50`, every job passed and all 26 projects were affected. Each job is measured from its own start, so queue time does not count:\n\n| Job | Duration | Within 1,620 s? |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| lint | 928 s | yes |\n| test-linux | 1,568 s | yes |\n| test-macos | 1,330 s | yes |\n| test-windows | 1,756 s | no, 136 s over; accepted by the ruling, inside the 1,800 s timeout |\n\n**test-windows across this round's runs:** 1,817 s cancelled ([41129896104](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41129896104)), 1,817 s cancelled ([41140769112](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41140769112)), 1,827 s cancelled ([41147797634](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41147797634)), 1,816 s cancelled ([41154845482](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41154845482)), 1,691 s ✓ ([41158752267](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41158752267)), 1,817 s cancelled ([41164516850](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41164516850)), 1,445 s ✓ ([41167084063](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41167084063)), 1,717 s ✓ ([41170499807](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41170499807)), 1,309 s ✓ ([41173425134](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41173425134)), 1,743 s ✓ ([41181558361](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41181558361)), 1,756 s ✓ ([41183966027](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41183966027)). Its successful runs span 1,309–1,756 s. The cancelled runs hit the 30-minute timeout on earlier heads; their causes and fixes are in the sections below.\n\nThe history below was written before the ruling. Where it says no run has every job at or under 1,620 s, that is still true. Under the ruling, that is no longer the bar.\n\n---\n## Every hosted run this round: each job from its own start\n\nThe bar is 1,620 s per job. All 26 projects are affected on every run. \"Over\" marks a job above the bar.\n\n| Run | Head | lint | test-linux | test-windows | test-macos |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| [41129896104](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41129896104) | `f3932af4` | 943 s ✓ | 1,542 s failed | 1,817 s cancelled, over | 1,423 s ✓ |\n| [41140769112](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41140769112) | `0d9f1778` | 977 s ✓ | 1,669 s failed, over | 1,817 s cancelled, over | 1,422 s ✓ |\n| [41147797634](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41147797634) | `17b88c96` | 737 s ✓ | 523 s failed | 1,827 s cancelled, over | 1,558 s ✓ |\n| [41154845482](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41154845482) | `f40199bd` | 940 s ✓ | 1,707 s failed, over | 1,816 s cancelled, over | 1,459 s ✓ |\n| [41158752267](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41158752267) | `5d931a94` | 931 s ✓ | 1,508 s ✓ | 1,691 s ✓, over | 1,016 s ✓ |\n| [41164516850](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41164516850) | `bc60cc9c` | 954 s ✓ | 1,390 s ✓ | 1,817 s cancelled, over | 1,351 s ✓ |\n| [41167084063](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41167084063) | `2b2757b6` | 978 s ✓ | 1,804 s cancelled, over | 1,445 s ✓ | 1,186 s ✓ |\n| [41170499807](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41170499807) | `b89c92de` | 901 s ✓ | 1,809 s cancelled, over | 1,717 s ✓, over | 630 s ✓ |\n| [41173425134](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41173425134) | `6110bd8f` | 885 s ✓ | 1,758 s ✓, over | 1,309 s ✓ | 967 s ✓ |\n| [41181558361](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41181558361) | `1ae3b4f9` | 742 s ✓ | 1,474 s ✓ | 1,743 s ✓, over | 1,582 s failed |\n| [41183966027](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41183966027) | `911aba50` | 928 s ✓ | 1,568 s ✓ | 1,756 s ✓, over | 1,330 s ✓ |\n\n**Why each red run failed:**\n\n| Run | Failure | Fixed by |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 41129896104 | test-linux's coverage floor, under the earlier arrangement | the ruling, option (ii) |\n| 41140769112 | the same | the same |\n| 41147797634 | test-linux's runner ran out of disk | `f40199bd` |\n| 41154845482 | test-linux: test 19's bound under contention | the shared release build, `0d1527a6` |\n| 41181558361 | test-macos: `check-warm-rust-cache`'s doubled slash | `bd0e15eb` |\n\nEvery other failure is a 30-minute cancellation, with every phase green up to it.\n\n**The head now in review is `911aba50`.** On it, every job passed and lint, test-linux and test-macos were under the bar; test-windows took 1,756 s. No run yet has every job at or under 1,620 s.\n\n---\n## Run [41183966027](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41183966027) (`911aba50`): all four jobs pass; test-windows is 136 s over the bar\n\nAll 26 projects are affected. Each job measured from its own start:\n\n| Job | Duration | Result | Within 1,620 s? |\n| --- | ---: | --- | --- |\n| lint | 928 s | ✓ | yes |\n| test-macos | 1,330 s | ✓ | yes |\n| test-linux | 1,568 s | ✓ | yes |\n| test-windows | 1,756 s | ✓ | **no, 136 s over** |\n\n- **test-linux:** test 19 took 354 s and test 23 801 s, with its crate runs at 516 s and 98 s. This pull request cannot reach the new crate-publication warm, so these figures are runner variance, not the cut.\n- **test-windows:** main test phase 26:42; distribution-test 21:45, with its release build at 474 s and packaging at 442 s. `check-relative-interpreter` took 135 s, again during that release build.\n\n**Fixed since the previous run:**\n- `check-warm-rust-cache` failed test-macos on run 41181558361, because macOS's `TMPDIR` ends in a slash. Fixed in `bd0e15eb`.\n- The version inventory now refuses a directory it cannot read, rather than skipping it (`911aba50`), with an enforced case that failed without the fix.\n\n**Not established:** a run with every job at or under 1,620 s. Across this round's runs, test-windows took 1,309–1,756 s and test-linux 1,474–1,809 s.\n\n---\n## Crate-publication warm (a), manager replies 258 and 259, head `1ae3b4f9`\n\n**(b), answered from run 41173425134's own log.** test-linux restores the Rust cache on a full match (`v0-rust-test-linux-Linux-x64-2c443741-d76682ad`). rust-cache v2.9.3 saves only when a key misses, so no pull-request run here ever saves a cache: every run restores the main warmer's, and nothing built `target/crate-publication` into it. rust-cache's own cleanup (`cleanTargetDir` recursing into non-profile directories) keeps a nested target directory's dependency units.\n\n**(a), built in this change.**\n- The warmer's Linux leg makes the crate publication's packaging verification build: `cargo package` of every publishable crate, with `RUSTFLAGS=-D warnings`, into `target/crate-publication`.\n- `pr-build-commands.sh` lists that build, and `check-warm-rust-cache.sh` and both reuse checks hold the warmer to it.\n- The publishable set is read from `cargo metadata`, and equals `CRATE_PUBLICATION_ORDER`.\n\n**Measured locally.**\n- The added warm step takes **360 s**.\n- After it, `cargo publish --dry-run` of every crate in publication order, in that directory and with those RUSTFLAGS, compiled **no crate outside the workspace**, in 219 s together:\n\n  | Crate | Time |\n  | --- | ---: |\n  | plugin-api | 2 s |\n  | in-memory | 11 s |\n  | local-md | 12 s |\n  | linear | 18 s |\n  | github-projects | 18 s |\n  | status-options | 24 s |\n  | core | 36 s |\n  | onetaskgraph | 98 s |\n\n- A cold verification of `onetaskgraph-in-memory` compiled 42 non-workspace crates in 34 s, against 0 crates and 11 s after the warm.\n- `check-warm-rust-cache-reuse.sh` run whole (the real warmer, then test-linux's builds) passes, as do `check-warm-rust-cache-reuse-real.sh` and `check-warm-rust-cache.sh`.\n\n**Unmeasured here.** The effect on test 23 reaches pull requests only once this is on main and the main warmer has run, because a pull request restores only caches saved on its own ref or on main. It is for the onetaskgraph-ci-budgets step to measure after merge.\n\nThe warm job's `timeout-minutes` (60) is unchanged; main's last Linux warm took 13 min.\n\n---\n## Run [41173425134](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41173425134) (`6110bd8f`): all four jobs pass; test-linux is over the bar\n\nAll 26 projects are affected. Each job measured from its own start:\n\n| Job | Duration | Result | Within 1,620 s? |\n| --- | ---: | --- | --- |\n| lint | 885 s | ✓ | yes |\n| test-macos | 967 s | ✓ | yes |\n| test-windows | 1,309 s | ✓ | yes |\n| test-linux | 1,758 s | ✓ | **no** |\n\n**Test 23's phases** (L1, manager reply 257), from this run's `ONETASKGRAPH_CD_TIMINGS` lines:\n\n| Phase | Time |\n| --- | ---: |\n| `release-publish publish --to crates`, refused part way | 689.1 s |\n| `release-publish publish --to crates`, the re-run that sends the rest | 125.8 s |\n| `release-publish build` | 36.5 s |\n| about 110 `available` / `verify` / `publish` calls against the registry doubles | about 170 s, 1–61 s each |\n| **test 23 in all** | **1,020 s** |\n\nThe two crate runs are `cargo publish`'s packaging verification. It builds each crate once, in `target/crate-publication`, under its own RUSTFLAGS. That is the release's real path, and the test must keep driving it.\n\n**Windows, `check-relative-interpreter` (W):**\n- Its 136 s on run 41170499807 lines up exactly with `scripts:distribution-test`'s release build: it started at 05:18:26, distribution-test ended at 05:20:14, and it finished at 05:20:43.\n- On run 41167084063 it started after distribution-test had ended, and took 50 s.\n- Locally, beside a distribution journey that was mid-build, its targeted pass took 23 s with no wait.\n- So the cause is CPU contention on the 4-core runner, not anything in the guard. I have cut nothing there.\n\n---\n## Windows `scripts:test` cut (w1, manager reply 256), heads `b89c92de` and `cbbbcc5c`\n\n**How it was timed.** test-windows points `BASH_ENV` at `scripts/script-start-log.sh`. Each guard `scripts:test` starts announces itself on standard error, which the job log timestamps, and records its start; nested guards and other tasks are not recorded. `ci-job.sh` adds the figures to its closing line. `scripts/test_ci_timings.py` drives the recorder and both summaries.\n\n**Two causes found:**\n- **The version inventory walked build output.** `product_versions.discover_product_version_files` globbed the whole tree five times, `target/`, `node_modules` and `.venv` included. It now walks once and skips `.git`, `node_modules`, `target` and `.venv`. It finds the same 21 files in 0.1 s instead of 4.3 s, and `check-workspace-config.sh` takes 0.2 s instead of 15.4 s locally. New enforced cases refuse an unowned version constant and accept one under each skipped directory; that case failed with the pruning removed.\n- **The enforced check copied files one process at a time.** `check-workspace-config-enforced` started a `mkdir` and a `cp` for each of the 1,292 tracked files. It now uses `copy_tracked_files`' single tar stream, and takes 9.8 s instead of 31.5 s locally.\n\n**Windows per-guard time, before (run 41167084063) and after (run 41170499807):**\n\n| Guard | Before | After |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| check-workspace-config-enforced | 146 s | **46 s** |\n| check-relative-interpreter | 50 s | 136 s |\n| check-python-call-sites-enforced | 42 s | 75 s |\n| check-file-lock, with the Python tests after it | 39 s | 45 s |\n| check-live-lane-selection | 22 s | 29 s |\n| every other guard | 0–12 s | 0–13 s |\n| **scripts:test in all** | **351 s** | **399 s** |\n\nEvery other guard was 1.1–1.7× slower on the after-run, which reads as a slower runner. `check-relative-interpreter`'s 2.7× is larger than that, and its cause is not established.\n\n**Run [41170499807](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41170499807) (`b89c92de`), all 26 projects affected:**\n\n| Job | Duration | Result |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| lint | 901 s | ✓ |\n| test-macos | 630 s | ✓ |\n| test-windows | 1,717 s | ✓, over 1,620 s |\n| test-linux | 1,809 s | cancelled at 30 min |\n\n- **test-linux:** every phase was green, and budgets finished at 05:25:33, as the job was cancelled. The main test phase's critical path is `github-workflows:cd-journeys` at 27 min 37 s, against 20 min 49 s on the passing run. Inside it, release-cd test 23 (\"the publish job waits for every platform, then publishes the crates, SDKs and launcher once\") took 987 s, against 700 s; test 19 took 406 s against its 900 s bound.\n- **Run [41167084063](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41167084063) (`2b2757b6`):** test-windows passed at 1,445 s, and test-linux was cancelled at 30 min the same way.\n\n**Fixed afterwards in `cbbbcc5c`:** the stricter trace summary from `bc60cc9c` threw away Windows' distribution timing on these runs, because background subshells interleave their trace lines. It now reads the trace in time order.\n\n**No run has yet had every job at or under 1,620 s.** The one with all four jobs passing, 41158752267, had test-windows at 1,691 s.\n\n---\n## Run [41158752267](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41158752267) on `5d931a94`: all four jobs pass; Windows is 71 s over the bar\n\nAll 26 projects are affected. Each job measured from its own start:\n\n| Job | Duration | Result | Within 1,620 s? |\n| --- | ---: | --- | --- |\n| lint | 931 s | ✓ | yes |\n| test-macos | 1,016 s | ✓ | yes |\n| test-linux | 1,508 s | ✓ | yes |\n| test-windows | 1,691 s | ✓ | **no, 71 s over** |\n\n**test-linux:** coverage passed the 92% floor, test 19 took 346 s against its 900 s bound, and budgets passed. Phase times, all concurrent:\n\n| Phase | Time |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Crate coverage | 19:35 |\n| SDK coverage | 8:17 |\n| Linear test | 7:26 |\n| Main test | 23:40 |\n| distribution-check | 5:54 |\n| distribution-test | 17:22 |\n| budgets | 0:19 |\n\n**The two cuts in this round, every case kept:**\n- **`0d1527a6`:** the Python SDK's installed-wheel journey builds `onetaskgraph-cli` for the host triple, under the cli-wheel lock. It reuses the release build that the distribution journey and test 19 share, instead of compiling a third one beside them. Locally, after that build, the journey takes 9 s and compiles nothing.\n- **`5d931a94`:** the two SDK tests that each ran the real generator, about a minute apiece, share one session-scoped regeneration. Locally the suite went from 227 s to 147 s.\n\nOn test-linux, `sdk-python`'s pytest went from 1,208 s to 287 s.\n\n**test-windows**, phase times:\n\n| Phase | Time |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Main test | 25:36 |\n| Linear test | 18:18 |\n| distribution-check | 1:27 |\n| distribution-test | 20:16 |\n\n- **Release build:** `test-distribution.sh:146` took 508 s, and packaging (`:458`) 320 s.\n- **e2e suite:** its 717 tests took 342 s and finished at 02:34:22.\n- **The tail:** `scripts:test`, which started at 02:31:20 once a slot freed and ran about 7 minutes to 02:38:17.\n\n**Not yet established:** one run with every job at or under 1,620 s. Windows is the remaining overrun.\n\n---\n## Hosted runs of the ruling arrangement\n\n**Run [41147797634](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41147797634) (`17b88c96`).** test-linux's runner ran out of disk after 523 s (\"No space left on device\"; no job log). Fixed in `f40199bd`: a test-linux step removes the runner image's unused SDKs in the background. The next run then had 86 GB free before building.\n\n**Run [41154845482](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41154845482) (`f40199bd`).** Each job measured from its own start:\n\n| Job | Duration | Result |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| lint | 940 s | ✓ |\n| test-macos | 1,459 s | ✓ |\n| test-linux | 1,707 s | failed |\n| test-windows | 1,816 s | cancelled at 30 min |\n\n- **Coverage:** `workspace:coverage` passed the 92% floor, so option (ii) holds coverage. Budgets passed.\n- **test-linux** failed on release-cd **test 19** at its 900 s bound (901.6 s). Its tests 20–27 and 29 failed with it, depending on its output. When the bound fired, three release builds of the binary were alive beside the instrumented coverage builds: test 19's own, the distribution journey's, and the Python SDK wheel test's `maturin pep517` build.\n- **test-linux phase times, all concurrent:**\n\n  | Phase | Time |\n  | --- | ---: |\n  | Crate coverage | 23 min 14 s |\n  | SDK coverage | 23 min 45 s, including `sdk-python:coverage` at 20 min 15 s (pytest 1,208 s) |\n  | Linear test | 8 min 39 s |\n  | Main test | 25 min 25 s, failed at test 19 |\n  | distribution-check | 6 min 19 s |\n  | distribution-test | 27 min 18 s |\n  | budgets | 5 s |\n\n- **test-windows:** the e2e suite finished at 01:48:09, and the job was cancelled at 01:50:22. Its release build (`test-distribution.sh:146`) took 522 s, slower than any earlier run (278–497 s).\n\n**No passing timing run is established.**\n\n---\n## The user's coverage ruling, option (ii): heads `f077f63f` and `17b88c96`\n\nThe e2e suites are measured again. Only the live crates' own suites (`onetaskgraph-github-projects` and `onetaskgraph-linear`) run uninstrumented, every case in `test`. The line-coverage floor is **92%** over the instrumented suites.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **The four e2e crates are back to their 54327de7 form.** Their `coverage` runs `scripts/rust-coverage.sh`, `workspace:coverage` depends on them, and test-linux exports `ONETASKGRAPH_TESTS_UNDER_COVERAGE=1` again, so the live e2e suites' `test` runs only their credentialed journeys and coverage runs the rest.\n- **`scripts/live-crate-test.sh`** runs a crate tagged `coverage:uninstrumented` whole, even under that variable. `check-ci-recipes.sh` drives that arm, and its refusal of a crate with no `project.json`, against its stand-in `cargo`.\n- **The GitHub Projects plugin's `coverage`** now builds its tests instrumented and runs none (`rust-coverage.sh --build-only`), because its e2e suite's measured entry-point pairs run them. With a filter selecting no test, it writes no profile: 0 after the build, checked locally. `check-coverage-enforced.sh` accepts only a no-op or that build-only step, depending on `workspace:coverage-clear` and uncached, for a tagged crate. Its `--no-report` and `nextest` checks are now anchored on the crate run, after `check-workspace-config-enforced.sh` showed the build-only line would otherwise satisfy them.\n- **The floor is 92** in `rust-coverage.sh`, `check-coverage-enforced.sh` and `AGENTS.md`.\n\n**Measured union:** 92.69% on `54327de7` (60,264 of 65,015 lines). Per crate, see the table below; github-projects, linear, live and local-md are below 95%, but the floor is the total.\n\n**Local checks, all passing:**\n- `check-ci-recipes`, `check-workspace-config-enforced`, `check-coverage-enforced`, `check-test-in-coverage`, `check-workspace-config`, `check-nx-graph`, `check-live-lane` and `check-warm-rust-cache`;\n- the telemetry, `budget_commands` and `lane_wiring` tests;\n- judged diff lint: 40 passed, 0 failed.\n\nA test-linux `--select-only` shows the plugin's build-only coverage pulled in by `onetaskgraph-github-projects-e2e:coverage`, the four e2e crates measured, and the live crates in `test` only.\n\n## Windows experiment (manager reply 250): reverted\n\n`0d9f1778` gave `onetaskgraph-e2e` its own concurrent phase on test-windows, with the shared phase cut from 3 slots to 2. Measured on run [41140769112](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41140769112):\n- the suite started 4:35 after bootstrap, against 11:42 and 17:53 before;\n- its 717 tests took 912 s, against 429 s on the passing run;\n- the shared phase's last tasks started no earlier (+20:54, against +20:13);\n- the job was cancelled at 30 minutes as before.\n\nIt is reverted in `e936ce9e`.\n\nThe overrun on f3932af4 is the runner draw. On the same image, cache key and workload, identical work ran 1.4–3.9× slower than on run 41106804596 (1,496 s): the release build took 497 s against 278 s, and a git checkout 27 s against 7 s.\n\n---\n## Partial instrumentation, measured on `54327de7` (answering the planner's question; nothing changed)\n\nThe committed arrangement is unchanged: the e2e and live crates' suites run uninstrumented. The question was whether instrumenting only one of the two sets would bring coverage back to the floor. This section answers it.\n\n**How it was measured.** A scratch clone at `54327de7` with its own target directory, so the worktree's existing profiles were not touched. Every crate's measured command was `cargo llvm-cov --no-report nextest --package <crate> --all-features --locked`, as `scripts/rust-coverage.sh` runs it, plus `--no-fail-fast`. Crates ran one at a time with credentials cleared. Each set's profiles were kept apart, and each union was reported over hard links to them.\n\n| Union | Lines covered | Line coverage | vs the 95% floor |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| A: the 10 baseline crates (committed) | 33,579 / 65,015 | 51.65% | 28,186 lines short |\n| A+E: plus the 4 e2e suites | 60,264 / 65,015 | **92.69%** | 1,501 lines short |\n| A+L: plus the 2 live crates' suites | 44,094 / 65,015 | **67.82%** | 17,671 lines short |\n| A+E+L: everything (cross-check) | 62,249 / 65,015 | 95.75% | passes; matches the earlier full union |\n\nA is 51.65% here, not the earlier 52.18%, because the live crates' test files are built in this clone and so count in the denominator.\n\n**Per crate** (an asterisk marks a crate below 95%):\n\n| Crate | A | A+E | A+L | A+E+L |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| onetaskgraph | 7.31%* | 95.79% | 7.31%* | 95.79% |\n| core | 74.17%* | 95.66% | 74.17%* | 95.66% |\n| e2e-support | 26.81%* | 96.53% | 26.81%* | 96.53% |\n| github-live | 94.42%* | 96.12% | 96.12% | 96.12% |\n| github-projects | 7.34%* | 82.59%* | 72.49%* | 94.53%* |\n| in-memory | 98.56% | 98.89% | 98.56% | 98.89% |\n| linear | 3.27%* | 81.44%* | 84.61%* | 96.55% |\n| live | 94.28%* | 94.28%* | 98.04% | 98.04% |\n| live-janitor | 95.38% | 95.38% | 95.38% | 95.38% |\n| local-md | 94.06%* | 94.50%* | 94.06%* | 94.50%* |\n| plugin-api | 92.54%* | 97.05% | 93.62%* | 97.05% |\n| status-options | 81.67%* | 100.00% | 81.67%* | 100.00% |\n\n**Failed runs.**\n- `onetaskgraph-github-projects-e2e` failed 22 of 406 tests, and `onetaskgraph-linear-e2e` failed 1 of 167.\n- All 23 are setup failures of the unbootstrapped scratch clone:\n  - SDK journeys: `ModuleNotFoundError: onetaskgraph_sdk`;\n  - `lane_wiring`: needs real Nx;\n  - 13 `live_pair` tests: \"compile onetaskgraph-github-projects --test … before this pair\", because the live crate ran after the e2e set;\n  - two `human_reporters` tests, which also fail locally in the bootstrapped worktree, so they are a host failure.\n- Their lines are missing from A+E and A+E+L. The cross-check bounds that loss: A+E+L is 95.75%, the earlier full union's total. The largest per-crate difference is 0.24 points, in github-live.\n\n**Measured cost** (local, cold instrumented target, sequential, one crate at a time; build and listing = wall clock minus nextest's own test time):\n\n| Set | Wall clock | Tests | Instrumented build and listing |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Base (A) | 955 s | 76 s | 879 s |\n| E2E suites (E), on top of A | 1,711 s | ≥1,180 s (lower bound: the failures above ended early) | 531 s |\n| Live crates (L), on top of A | 309 s | 57 s | 252 s |\n\nE is about 85% of what instrumenting both sets adds, and L about 15%.\n\n**Hosted figures** (each job from its own start):\n- With E and L instrumented, run 41095685030 on 6756beb7 passed test-linux at 1,665 s, and run 41106804596 on 54327de7 was cancelled at 1,803 s by test 19's bound. Both had a 25–27 min coverage phase.\n- With neither instrumented, run 41129896104 on `f3932af4` took test-linux 1,542 s: the coverage phase took 6 min 40 s, and test 19 passed in 295.5 s.\n\n**Estimates, not measurements:**\n- **A+E:** about 85% of the old coverage phase returns, roughly 22–24 min, concurrent with the test phase. The CPU contention that took test 19 to about 900 s largely returns, so test 19 would take roughly 600–900 s and test-linux roughly 1,620–1,800 s. It is likely over the bar, and still 1,501 lines short of the floor.\n- **A+L:** a small instrumented build, about 250 s locally. The live session, which runs about 1,290 s and is paced by the network rather than the CPU, moves into the coverage phase. test-linux would take roughly 1,550–1,650 s and test 19 roughly 300–450 s. It is 17,671 lines short of the floor.\n\n## Hosted run [41129896104](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41129896104) on `f3932af4` (merge of main; all 26 of 26 Nx projects affected)\n\n| Job | Duration | Result |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| lint | 943 s | ✓ |\n| test-macos | 1,423 s | ✓ |\n| test-linux | 1,542 s | failed |\n| test-windows | 1,817 s | cancelled at the 30-minute timeout |\n\n- **test-linux's only failed task is `workspace:coverage`,** which measured 52.03% against the 95% floor. The floor stays at 95% pending the user's ruling. Every other phase passed: the e2e and live suites ran uninstrumented in `test`, distribution-check and distribution-test passed, and test 19 took 295.5 s against its 900 s bound. budgets did not run, because it follows a failed phase group.\n- **test-windows:** the `onetaskgraph-e2e` suite's 717-test binary started at 21:37:31 and was still running at the 30-minute cancellation. The same job passed in 1,496 s on run 41106804596 (54327de7). This run's overrun is not yet explained.\n- **This is not a passing timing run.** The one-run bar is not met on this head.\n\n---\n\n## Coverage change (user-approved lever (a), 2026-10-30), heads `ad0e4fe9` / `f3932af4`\n\nOn test-linux, the e2e suites (`onetaskgraph-e2e`, `-github-projects-e2e`, `-linear-e2e`, `-status-options-e2e`) and the live crates' suites (`onetaskgraph-github-projects`, `onetaskgraph-linear`) now run every case, credentialed journeys included, **uninstrumented** in `test`. They are tagged `coverage:uninstrumented`:\n- their `coverage` targets are no-ops;\n- `workspace:coverage` no longer depends on them;\n- the coverage phase excludes the tag, so no profile of theirs enters the union;\n- `scripts/check-coverage-enforced.sh` holds tag, target and aggregate to one another, and is watched refusing each mismatch.\n\nLine coverage and the **unchanged 95% floor** are computed over the remaining suites.\n\n**Measured unions** (local, every case run, the repository's own coverage targets and report, head `54327de7`):\n\n| Union | Lines covered | Line coverage |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Before: every suite instrumented | 62,252 / 65,015 | **95.75%** (floor passes) |\n| After: e2e and live suites uninstrumented | 33,579 / 64,349 | **52.18%** (below the floor) |\n\n28,673 lines in 90 files are covered only by those suites. Coverage by crate, before → after:\n- `onetaskgraph-github-projects`: 94.5% → 7.3% (9,612 lines)\n- `onetaskgraph-e2e-support`: 96.6% → 26.8% (5,966 lines)\n- `onetaskgraph-core`: 95.7% → 74.2% (5,867 lines)\n- `onetaskgraph-linear`: 96.5% → 3.3% (3,704 lines)\n- `onetaskgraph` (the binary): 95.8% → 7.3% (3,303 lines)\n- smaller losses in plugin-api, live, local-md, status-options and github-live\n\nLargest files: `github-projects/src/lib.rs` (7,424 lines), `e2e-support/src/fixtures.rs` (5,273), `linear/src/lib.rs` (2,726), `onetaskgraph/src/main.rs` (1,426), `core/src/engine/copy.rs` (1,417), `core/src/engine/copy/projections.rs` (904), `onetaskgraph/src/render.rs` (843).\n\n**test-linux's coverage floor therefore fails** until the user rules: lower the floor to what the remaining suites cover, or keep instrumenting the e2e and live suites. The manager has the figures (reply 247).\n\n---\n\n## Current head `54327de7`: status under the one-run bar\n\nThe bar is one all-project run with every job at or under 1,620 s, each measured from its own start. All 26 Nx projects are affected by this PR.\n\n| Run (head) | lint | test-linux | test-windows | test-macos |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| [41106804596](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41106804596) (`54327de7`) | 951 s ✓ | 1,803 s, cancelled (release-cd test 19 hit its 900 s bound mid-compile) | **1,496 s ✓** | **1,485 s ✓** |\n| [41095685030](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41095685030) (`6756beb7`) | 863 s ✓ | **1,665 s, passed** (45 s over the bar) | 1,835 s, cancelled | **799 s ✓** |\n| [41089773573](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41089773573) (`e09f2029`) | 969 s ✓ | 1,821 s, cancelled | 1,815 s, cancelled | 718 s, failed (fixed in `6756beb7`) |\n\n**The bar is not yet met in one run.** lint, test-windows and test-macos have each passed under 1,620 s. test-linux has passed only at 1,665 s, on the run whose test-19 bound was 22 min; that raise was reverted per manager reply 243. Its floor (coverage 25.7–27.3 min on a contended 4-core runner) is reported to the manager, together with what a coverage cut, a larger runner or another job would each buy.\n\n**Linux-only release guards.** `check-release-pr-sync`, `check-release-tooling-selection` and both `check-crate-sibling-resolution` guards skip only on Windows. Their production jobs (`release-plz.yml` → `release-plz`, and `release.yml` → `publish`) are ubuntu-latest only. They run in full in **test-linux** through `scripts:distribution-check`: the green test-linux in run 41095685030 ran them, within 1,665 s. `check-fixture-discrimination`, and its credential-free watch, run on Linux only (manager reply 242). `.github/policy/tests/inventory.test.mjs` derives every `distribution-check` and `distribution-test` guard that skips. It refuses a skip broader than Windows, or broader than off-Linux for a suite-level property, and refuses a test-linux that does not run both targets unconditionally.\n\n**Measured savings since the merge head** (hosted, per phase):\n- Windows `distribution-check`: 19m 32s → 1m 10s–1m 40s (the four guards moved).\n- macOS `distribution-test`: 23m 2s → 7m 15s (the fixture check moved).\n- Windows' packaging test: 374 s → 280 s, by failing each install-stage refusal at the first CLI and packaging the products once.\n- Linux release-cd test 20: it now starts the recipe at its packaging over test 19's wheel (`release-linux run --prebuilt`). It still asserts the recipe stops on the failing test-install before any publication, now asserts no release build ran, and refuses a prebuilt run with no wheel. Test 19 still drives the full run end to end.\n- Workflow suites: about 1,240 s of slot time per Windows run moved to Linux (`github-workflows` 519 s, `github-workflow-policy` 718 s); macOS too.\n\n**Fixed along the way:**\n- The release verifier read `target/` while the build wrote `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`, giving release-binary drift.\n- The SDK's `test-uncovered` (`check-sync.sh`) ran on no Linux phase. It now runs beside its coverage, and `check-ci-recipes` holds every such target.\n- `check-file-lock`'s lock-block pattern.\n- macOS `check-scoped-release-plz`, where cargo-binstall sits outside cargo's directory on that image.\n- The recorder's UTF-8-default assertion.\n\n**Remaining failure:** release-cd test 19 on test-linux. Its release compile, shared with the CI distribution journey through one build directory (locally the second build reuses all of it: 0 crates recompiled, 4 s), runs past the 15-minute bound under coverage contention. The bound then names the build processes alive.\n\nJudged diff lint: clean on `54327de7` (`20261030T182419Z-de912`).\n\n---\n\n## Current head `3fd525c7` (latest run [41066460202](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41066460202) pending)\n\n**Since `d7c8b98c`:**\n- **Workflow suites on Linux only (manager reply 239).** `github-workflows` and `github-workflow-policy` check workflow files whose production runs are Linux jobs, so `test-windows` and `test-macos` leave them to **test-linux**. `rust-cache.test` runs the real rust-cache action, which also runs on Windows and macOS runners, so it becomes its own target, `rust-cache-action`, and runs on all three. `.github/policy/tests/inventory.test.mjs` parses each job's real phases and refuses a suite leaving test-linux, or the rust-cache journey leaving a runner. The policy project is keyed on exactly the `distribution-check` guards it reads, and that list is held to `scripts/project.json`.\n- **release-cd test 19 (manager reply 240).** The diagnostic fired on `d7c8b98c`'s test-linux: `target/maturin` held **nothing**, and no other maturin was alive, only GitHub Projects cargo tests and a coverage run. Something empties the checkout's maturin staging; the writer is not yet named. In `3fd525c7` the release-cd journeys build into `target/release-cd` instead, sharing the checkout's `target` as cargo's build directory. The CLI-wheel lock, canonical binary path, wheel-extractor rule and journey lock follow the target directory cargo writes. Test 19 keeps both its builds in one staging area and one lock, which it still asserts. Locally, beside the CI-style distribution journey, it now takes 74 s, against 1,279 s waiting on CI before. All 50 CD journeys pass, as do `test_cli_wheel_lock.py` with its real concurrent wheels, the distribution-contract and packaging guards, and the distribution journey.\n- **Windows journey timing** is opt-in (`ONETASKGRAPH_DISTRIBUTION_TIMINGS=1`, set by test-windows) and printed as one line. On `31f3d82e` it showed `cd` into the checkout, the release wheel build's line, taking **406 s**, and `test-release-products.py` **374 s**.\n- **macOS recorder check** asserted the interpreter's UTF-8 default; it now asserts what `-W` and `-X` delivered. The earlier version is shown failing under `PYTHONUTF8=1`.\n- Judged diff lint: clean on `3fd525c7` (`20261030T134858Z-cad74`).\n\n**Hosted job durations, measured from each job's own start, with queue wait shown separately:**\n\n| Run (head) | lint | test-linux | test-windows | test-macos |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| [41057046328](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41057046328) (`31f3d82e`) | 863 s ✓ | waited 4,108 s; running | 1,852 s, cancelled at timeout | **1,630 s ✓** |\n| [41045867642](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41045867642) (`d7c8b98c`) | 841 s ✓ | waited 7,832 s; 1,782 s, failed (test 19) | 1,815 s, cancelled | 1,509 s, failed (recorder check, fixed) |\n\nOn `31f3d82e`, Windows' test+budgets phase finished in 17m 19s and distribution-check in 1m 26s. distribution-test ran 27m 33s, so the distribution journey is now the job's long pole.\n\n**Not established:** three consecutive all-project runs at ≤1,620 s per job. Windows still overruns, its distribution journey being the long pole. macOS is 10 s over. test-linux has not completed green since the Linux fixes. The Linear queue adds hours of waiting before test-linux starts; a follow-up proposes a remote lease, described for the user and not applied here.\n\n---\n\n## Current head `d7c8b98c`\n\n**Release guards moved off Windows (manager reply 237 and its conditions).** Four `scripts:distribution-check` guards now skip on Windows only, with a printed reason. Each drives code whose production job is Linux-only:\n\n| Guard | Production job it models (Linux-only) | Runs in full in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `scripts/check-release-pr-sync.sh` | `release-plz.yml` → `release-plz` (ubuntu-latest), `prepare-release-pr.sh` | **test-linux** (and test-macos) |\n| `scripts/check-release-tooling-selection.sh` | `release-plz.yml` → `release-plz` (ubuntu-latest), `select-release-version.sh` | **test-linux** (and test-macos) |\n| `scripts/check-crate-sibling-resolution.sh` | `release.yml` → `publish` (ubuntu-latest), `crate-sibling-resolution.sh` | **test-linux** (and test-macos) |\n| `scripts/check-crate-sibling-resolution-same-second.sh` | `release.yml` → `publish` (ubuntu-latest) | **test-linux** (and test-macos) |\n\n`test-linux` runs `scripts:distribution-check` unconditionally (`1 run scripts:distribution-check -` in `scripts/ci-job.sh`). `.github/policy/tests/inventory.test.mjs` holds this from the tree, not from a list. It derives every `distribution-check` guard that skips Windows, and requires:\n- each one's skip to match Windows alone;\n- `test-linux` to run on `ubuntu-latest` through `just ci-test-linux`;\n- that recipe's phases to run `distribution-check` unconditionally.\n\nIt is watched refusing a skip widened to `*linux*`, a `test-linux` that drops the phase, and a `test-linux` moved to `windows-latest`. Per manager reply 238 it lives in the policy project rather than `ci-contexts`, so a guard-script edit does not run the CD journeys. Guards whose behaviour depends on the platform, such as `check-release-packaging` and `check-release-probe`, still run on Windows. `distribution-check` passes in full locally with the skips in place.\n\n**Other changes since `9a2e38ec`:**\n- `test-release-products.py` packages the products once and restores them from a kept copy for the final verification; every refusal is still asserted.\n- release-cd test 19's failure on `test-linux` (maturin finding `target/maturin/onetaskgraph-source` missing) is **not yet explained**. maturin 1.14.1 renames each artifact into `target/maturin/` while it builds, so a second maturin over the same target directory outside the CLI-wheel lock would take the staged file away. Every maturin build in the tree takes that lock, and the test passes locally in each configuration tried: alone, beside the distribution journey under the relative-interpreter check, and with no staging directory. A failed CLI-wheel build now prints what `target/maturin` holds and every live maturin and cargo process, and test 19 asserts that output. The next hosted occurrence will therefore name the other writer.\n- Judged diff lint is clean on `d7c8b98c` (`20261030T110728Z-54ea3`).\n\n---\n\n## Current head `b5224c4f`: merge of main `e2d3bf08`, then three commits\n\n**Merge (`e7beeb88`, parents `ef1322ea` and `e2d3bf08`).** Main brought its Rust cache warmer (#3825), the Linear live-document policy (#3821) and human-task projection (#3824). How the conflicts were resolved:\n- The pull request's live credentials sit on `test-linux`. That job therefore carries `ONETASKGRAPH_LINEAR_DOCUMENT_MODE: ordinary` and joins the `linear-live-documents` concurrency group. `check`'s Linux leg joins the group only on a push.\n- The warmer keeps this branch's `warm-macos` leg, skipped on a release merge. It gains main's reuse check and the reporting of a failed build.\n- `.github/tests/fixtures/ci-speedup-test-jobs.yml` is deleted. The warmer test reads `ci.yml`'s `test-linux`, `test-windows` and `test-macos` and `scripts/ci-job.sh` alone, and expects three legs.\n- `pr-build-commands.sh` understands `live-crate-test.sh` test targets.\n- `distribution-diagnostic` removes floating stable before its cache step.\n\n**Changed assertion of #3821 (manager reply 232).** `.github/linear-documents-policy/policy.test.mjs` asserted that editing the `justfile` or either Linear crate's `project.json` selects no `github-workflows` publication journey. That cannot hold on this branch. The CD journeys really read the `justfile` and every crate, `.github/test-inputs.mjs` declares those reads, and `ci-contexts` enforces them. So that half was removed. What still holds:\n- those edits select `linear-documents-policy`;\n- an edit inside `.github/linear-documents-policy/` alone selects that project and not `github-workflows`;\n- both the push leg's and `test-linux`'s credentialed steps carry the ordinary document mode and the shared queue.\n\n**Policy journeys split into their own project (manager reply 233; judged lint `expensive_tests_stay_behind_their_own_edge`).** Before this, a crate or SDK edit selected `github-workflows`' CD journeys, and that also ran the policy journeys, which took 112 s locally. The eight policy journeys, their fixture, `budgets.yaml` and the macOS budget reporter now live under `.github/policy/`, as the project `github-workflow-policy`. Nx assigns a file to the project whose root holds it, so the move is what makes the split work. Budget ids, labels and thresholds are unchanged. `ci-contexts` reconciles both projects' read sets and runs every test file exactly once between them. It also drives real Nx:\n- a crate edit selects `github-workflows` alone;\n- a policy edit selects `github-workflow-policy` alone;\n- a workflow edit selects both.\n\nBefore the move, that test failed with \"policy-routing.test.mjs: github-workflows\", which is the failure it exists to catch.\n\n**Recorder fix (judged lint).** `scripts/python-call-site.sh` looped forever on a lone `-W` or `-X`. It now records the option and leaves the interpreter to refuse it. `check-python-call-sites-enforced.sh` drives `-m`, `-W`, `-X` and the lone options through a real probe shim. Against the old recorder it fails with \"a lone -W hung the recorder\".\n\n**Checks on `b5224c4f`.**\n- Real Nx: `github-workflows` and `github-workflow-policy` format-check, lint, `ci-jobs` (9), `ci-contexts` (6), `policy-tests` (62) and `budgets`, plus `linear-documents-policy:test` (3).\n- Typecheck.\n- Scripts: `check-warm-rust-cache`, `check-warm-rust-cache-reuse-enforced`, `check-live-lane`, `check-live-lane-selection`, `check-ci-recipes`, `check-nx-graph`, `check-affected-selection`, `check-python-call-sites-enforced`, `check-live-janitor`, `check-test-in-coverage`, `check-live-seat-serialised` and `check-contract-inventory`. The last four ran on `e7beeb88`; the later commits change nothing they read.\n- Judged diff lint: 40 passed, 0 failed (`20261030T071024Z-d4eaf`).\n\n**Hosted run on `e7beeb88`** ([41015039043](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/41015039043)); every project is affected:\n\n| Job | Hosted duration | Note |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| lint | 1,517 s, success | within 1,620 s |\n| test-windows | 1,809 s, cancelled at the timeout | Full match of main's warmed cache. The Nx test+budgets phase succeeded in 27m 3s across all 24 projects; the job was cancelled about 4 s before ci-job finished. `scripts:distribution-test` ran beside it and took 24m 51s. `onetaskgraph-linear:test` is `parallelism: false`, so it started last, at +1,597 s. |\n| test-macos | 1,851 s, cancelled at the timeout | Cold: no `test-macos` cache exists until `warm-macos` runs on main. |\n| test-linux | not yet started | Queued in the `linear-live-documents` group behind the release-plz PR's `check (ubuntu-latest)`. |\n\n**The amendment's three consecutive all-project runs at ≤1,620 s are not established, and cannot be from this branch alone.** `test-macos` stays cold until this change is on main. `test-windows` overran by seconds even on a warm cache. The measurements have gone to the manager for a ruling.\n\n---\n\n## Current head `ef1322ea` (merge of main `1af42438`): state and evidence\n\n**Merge.** `ef1322ea` concludes the merge of main `1af42438`. Two files were added on both sides: `scripts/cli_wheel_lock.py` and `scripts/test_cli_wheel_lock.py` take main's copies (#3817), which contain everything the branch's copies did. `scripts/project.json` and `scripts/test-distribution.sh` keep the branch's text, which already runs main's lock. `python3 scripts/test_cli_wheel_lock.py` passed on the resolved tree (3 tests, 193 s, including the concurrent real-wheel builds). Judged diff lint on `ef1322ea` passed with 39 rules and 0 findings (`20261029T172920Z-612cb`, run with `LLMLINT_ONEHARNESS_BIN` unset because the configured wrapper path does not exist on this host).\n\n**Hosted runs of the two latest heads.** Both are cold or partially warm. No warm run exists yet.\n\n| Job | `3edec0fe`, [run 40767498380](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40767498380) | `ef1322ea`, [run 40906611848](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40906611848) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| lint | 723 s, success | 1,461 s, success |\n| test-linux | 1,818 s, cancelled at timeout (PR-ref Rust cache hit, 790 MB) | 1,852 s, cancelled (no Rust cache) |\n| test-windows | **1,518 s, success** (PR-ref Rust cache hit, 423 MB) | 1,848 s, cancelled (no Rust cache) |\n| test-macos | 1,912 s, cancelled (no Rust cache) | 1,856 s, cancelled (no Rust cache) |\n\nThe cache is the measured difference on Windows: the same job passed in 1,518 s with a restored PR-ref cache and timed out without one. The merge moved `Cargo.lock`'s hash, and the repository's 10 GB cache limit had already evicted this PR's earlier saves, so `ef1322ea` started cold on every OS. `rust-cache-warm.yml`, which saves these keys on every push to main, is on this branch only (404 on main). Per manager reply 223 it is being landed on main as a separate step, as #3813 was. No series of consecutive timing runs starts until it has warmed all three OSes. **The amendment's three consecutive all-project runs at ≤1,620 s on all four jobs are not yet established.**\n\n**Where the cold time goes (overlapping first-to-last output spans per project, not isolated costs).**\n- test-linux on `3edec0fe`: the tail is `github-workflows:cd-journeys`. Its `release-cd` file runs sequentially. \"the Linux recipe builds once…\" took 1,008 s and \"the publish job waits…\" took 438 s. The GitHub Projects live session took 898 s, of which ChangeToken was 795 s over 90 requests, in parallel with it. `distribution-test` took 22m19s. Test 19 and `distribution-test` share the CLI-wheel lock and the same cold release build.\n- Local measurements on this host (20 cores), `ef1322ea`:\n  - With dependencies warm, the release build of the workspace crates alone takes 95 s at `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4`. This is what the warmer leaves for test 19 and `distribution-test`.\n  - The `release-cd` file took 1,586 s, 1,007 s and 1,051 s on three consecutive runs. Timing each recipe invocation puts 451 s and 102 s in the two `cargo publish --to crates` invocations. Their verification builds compile about 246 units on every run, because the dependency fingerprints include the per-run `CARGO_HOME` source path, which names the double's random port. A warm cache cannot serve them. Making that path stable would change the release double, and is not applied.\n- test-windows on `ef1322ea` (cold): `scripts` (distribution) 1,600 s, `onetaskgraph:build` 1,587 s, github-projects-e2e 1,226 s, linear-e2e 896 s. On `3edec0fe` (cache hit): github-workflows 1,144 s, github-projects-e2e 1,006 s, linear-e2e 961 s, sdk-typescript 932 s, scripts 912 s.\n- test-macos (cold in both runs): `scripts` about 1,650 s, github-workflows about 1,550–1,670 s, `onetaskgraph:build` 1,057–1,504 s, live-janitor 1,397 s on `ef1322ea`. Crate test builds queue at Nx's default of 3 slots.\n\n**Linear.** The live capability journey failed on `3edec0fe` with `cannot resolve label otg-live-…: found 0 matches`. This is live Linear behaviour that another plan owns (manager reply 223). It is recorded here, not fixed, and no Linear case was skipped or weakened.\n\nNo coverage-reducing cut, timeout change, larger runner, job split or sampled case has been applied.\n\n---\n\n## Approved workflow-target input repair (verification running)\n\nPer manager reply 173, `github-workflows:test` aggregates `policy-tests` and `cd-journeys` in the same required job. The first retains the workflow graph and classifier reads (including `bun.lock`); the second retains the graph plus publication/build inputs. All ten `.test.mjs` files are assigned exactly once by a checked inventory. No case, platform, job, threshold, or rule is removed. Both targets remain uncached as before. The finished input guard passed; in independent scratch copies the actual guard exited 1 when the policy lockfile input or the CD CLI-wheel-lock input was omitted. The two real Nx targets and workflow format/lint/typecheck are running, as is judged diff lint.\n\nSuppression inventory correction: the earlier statement that no suppression was rewritten in `02c92fab` was too broad. That commit corrected the explanation string carrying the existing file-scoped `projects_stay_in_one_js_workspace` and `external_service_suite_stays_out_of_the_affected_tier` directives in `.github/project.json`: `default` became `cdJourneys` to name the actual input. Those rules and their scope were unchanged. Their reasons remain the required isolated private tooling package and real publication verification against public registry dependencies (loopback-only writes). Together with the three newly added helper-group explanations listed below, these are the suppression sites this resume writes. No other suppression change is claimed.\n\n## Local final head `df40bfb2`: ownership ruling and suppression inventory\n\nManager reply 170 approved exactly three block-scoped `code_lands_in_the_domain_that_owns_it` explanations in `crates/onetaskgraph-e2e-support/src/fixtures.rs`, with no rule or configuration change:\n\n- `GitHubBoardFields::served_count` / `served_since` (line 1440): incremental reads of the loopback server's private board ledger belong on its handle; AGENTS.md places the shared server here.\n- `GitHubBoard::tallies` / `content_counted` (line 2970): counts and response rendering read the server's private items and comments and stay beside the response implementation shared by engine and plugin journeys.\n- `LinearWorkspace::served_count` / `served_since` / `answered_since` (line 6358): incremental reads expose the shared loopback server's private request/answer ledgers and cannot be implemented by an external plugin suite.\n\nThese are the only new suppression blocks this resume added; each end marker closes only its named helper group. The earlier statement that the boundary repair adds none remains true of `02c92fab`. Formatting and static lint validation passed (775 files, no invalid ignores). The final judged diff lint with LLMLINT_ONEHARNESS_BIN unset is pending on `df40bfb2`; earlier failed runs remain labelled below. This commit changes comments only, so behavioral checks cited on `02c92fab` remain applicable; judged lint is being rerun because it reads comments.\n\n## Follow-up lint state, local head `24829dfc`\n\nCold hosted lint passed on published `02c92fab` in 770 seconds ([job](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40761148474/job/123073210619)). Its three test jobs have no completed passing result yet. This is not warm evidence.\n\nTwo later comment-only commits keep the behavioral evidence from `02c92fab`: `bde270df` condenses duplicated interpreter-policy comments and `24829dfc` restores the HTTP-status documentation to the test it describes. Shell syntax and Linear crate formatting passed respectively. Local judged lint has not yet passed this final head: `20261028T201953Z-c4693` found the duplicated comments, `20261028T202555Z-a0692` found the misplaced status comment, and both were corrected. A separate run (`20261028T202436Z-e6f77`) flagged three existing helper groups in the shared loopback harness; manager ruling is pending because AGENTS.md:685–686 explicitly places loopback servers there. No placement change or suppression has been made pending that ruling.\n\n## Cold Windows cost detail, `4264c5b4`\n\nIn [run 40753580505](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40753580505), the largest completed Windows test groups were Linear e2e 755.02 s (143 cases), GitHub Projects e2e 649.90 s (324 cases), and general e2e 574.79 s (716 cases). Its release-profile build took 6m 57s, versus 14m 18s in the earlier cold control at `24ed9d93` ([40741930571](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40741930571)). Distribution-check finished in 20m 10s; distribution-test failed after 10m 36s at the Windows path boundary fixed in `02c92fab`. These groups overlap and must not be summed. The difference between cold draws is observed variability, not a measured saving from a cut. No current-head warm result exists; manager reply 164 explicitly labels run 40761148474 cold pending main's fixed-key warmer.\n\n## Windows boundary repair `02c92fab`\n\nThe real packaging journeys now quote paths only where the release recipes interpolate them through a shell; both full and targeted fixtures deliberately include spaces. The targeted interpreter pass spells `.exe` on Git Bash, uses the release driver's existing `host_target()` instead of a duplicated mapping, and the enforcement journey preserves the native suffix in its scratch tree. Its Linux-host simulation runs real packaging with Git Bash's extensionless file lookup: the explicit suffix passes, and deliberately removing it makes Python refuse the literal path. The unquoted recipe was separately observed failing on a space-containing path in a scratch copy before accepting the fix.\n\nThe final full distribution journey under relative-interpreter PATH and call-site comparison passed; the final enforcement journey, shell syntax, Biome for the corrected project-input explanation, and diff whitespace passed. Judged diff lint over this final tree is pending; an earlier invocation timed out in oneharness after 630 seconds with zero reported findings (`20261028T201132Z-49744`), which is an error, not a passing lint result. Published current-head [CI run 40761148474](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40761148474) is queued; no hosted run of `02c92fab` has completed. Earlier induced failures are historical evidence, not passing runs. No new suppression was added by this repair; the existing project-file directive explanation rewrite is inventoried above. The standing file-scoped suppressions were unchanged.\n\nPer manager reply 160, publish these fixes rather than keep waiting for the cold macOS leg: it never acquired a runner after 50 minutes 32 seconds (19:21:45–20:12:17 UTC); its step list is empty. Per replies 156–157, the cache-key fix is being landed separately so main can warm it; all current figures remain cold, no further timing micro-cuts or cheaper packaging profile is applied, and the Linear quota is with the user (no account data was deleted and live checks remain required).\n\n## Cold hosted measurements on `4264c5b4` (run 40753580505)\n\nThe cache-key fix has not merged, so the main-push warmer has not used it; these are cold, not warm results. No warm figure or three consecutive passing runs is claimed.\n\n| Job | Cold wall clock | Result |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| [test-linux](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40753580505/job/123046511262) | 1821 s | cancelled |\n| [lint](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40753580505/job/123046511283) | 903 s | success |\n| [test-macos](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40753580505/job/123046511409) | 3032 s queued; no execution | cancelled without a runner |\n| [test-windows](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40753580505/job/123046511561) | 1816 s | cancelled |\n\nWindows additionally reported a real distribution-path failure: a native backslash `--dist` passed through shell-interpolated recipe arguments became a different path; the relative-interpreter pass also selected an extensionless Windows executable path that Python could not open. Narrow fixes are undergoing fresh verification. Linux live capability and asset-copy tests were refused by Linear with `QUOTA_EXCEEDED` (maximum 1000 documents per team); account capacity recovery was escalated to the manager. No live case was skipped.\n\n## Resumed verification at `4264c5b4`\n\nThe planner-authorized shared CLI-wheel lock is preserved. Fresh checks on this exact clean tree passed: the lock subprocess failure/recovery test; the real concurrent release/distribution wheel journey (375.443 s); the full distribution journey under relative-interpreter PATH and its targeted call-site comparison; deliberately missed-call-site enforcement; 13 workflow/context tests; Biome checks for the changed workflow files; Python compilation; and judged diff lint (`20261028T191942Z-93c55`, 39 passed, zero findings). The initial lint attempt could not find the configured harness wrapper; the successful retry supplied the installed oneharness explicitly. A direct optional shellcheck invocation reported existing SC2015/SC2016 sites outside the preserved lock diff; those sites were not changed. No new suppression was added by this resume. Disabling POSIX locking in a scratch copy was observed making the concurrency test fail with FileExistsError at the exclusive active-build directory; the committed implementation passed.\n\nPublished draft [#3642](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/pull/3642); current-head [CI run 40753580505](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40753580505) started at 19:21 UTC. Current-head cold lint passed in 903 seconds ([job](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40753580505/job/123046511283)); the test jobs are still pending. Per manager reply 152, all these measurements are cold: the fixed cache key has not merged to main, so its main-push warmer cannot have populated that key. No warm hosted measurement is claimed. Cache-control run 40741930571 was still queued at 2026-10-28 19:21 UTC. Publication follows the latest planner direction to verify, publish, then measure; the older publication hold below is historical. Packaging profile and timeout/overrun policy remain unchanged. The three consecutive all-project hosted runs required by the amendment are not yet established.\n\n## Earlier evidence (historical; commit labels retained)\n\n## Committed local head `6f969353` — publication held for the cache-fix control\n\nFour validated pieces are committed: `40d825f2` reuses the registered projection setup, `79e96744` fixes Windows dep-info recognition, `a15f53df` drives staging/packaging through release recipes, and `6f969353` explains the opt-in timing diagnostic. The tree is clean. These commits are not yet published: manager reply 129 asks that [run 40741930571](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40741930571) finish first so it measures `24ed9d93` unchanged. No hosted run of `6f969353` has completed.\n\nThe projection cut keeps all 12 malformed-private-reference cases, all 17 malformed-anchor cases, every real CLI invocation, diagnostic, per-case project-tree snapshot, and the successful-copy recovery. The unchanged control took 4.440/5.287 s; the final cut, including its stronger home snapshots, took 1.714/1.521 s (parallel pair 5.289 → 1.717 s). Identical onevcs registration setup is shared only within each test. Per manager reply 132, the home comparison normalizes only advisory-lock PID contents and identity `observed_at` values refreshed by reads; all other bytes, including JSON whitespace, identity decisions, checkout records and rules, stay held. A deliberately added byte in registry.json was observed failing that exact comparison, then reverted. All 53 projection journeys passed, as did the e2e project's Nx format-check/lint/typecheck. No case was removed or sampled; hosted saving is not yet measured.\n\nThe dep-info test reproduced the old Windows install failure with backslash paths before the fix, then accepted POSIX and Windows records and refused a Windows record naming both the mutated copy and the real crate. Disabling the contamination check was observed making the new negative case fail; the check was restored. `shellcheck -x`, shell syntax checks, the Bash-3 portability and tar-drain guards, the 13 workflow/context guard tests, and git diff whitespace checks passed. The full distribution journey under the relative interpreter and its comparison against the targeted pass passed after both packaging paths changed to `just release-<platform> package`; the deliberately missed-call-site/unwritable-recorder enforcement passed too. Final judged lint over the complete prepared tree passed with 39 rules and zero findings (`20261028T183848Z-a5588`), immediately before these commits; committing changed no content.\n\nSuppression delta: `.github/workflows/ci.yml` adds one block-scoped `no_second_broader_sweep_over_an_already_gated_commit` explanation around the default-off workflow_dispatch diagnostic: it is an intentionally isolated timing control, never a pull-request gate, while old required contexts must remain. `scripts/check-fixture-discrimination-credential-free.sh` moves its existing one-line `work_goes_through_command_surface` directive onto the real subprocess invocation inside the new path-style loop; the reason remains that the test must observe the exact child environment, and a recipe wrapper would change that boundary. No enforcement setting or coverage threshold changed. Earlier suppression deltas are inventoried below.\n\n### Cache-fix control: `24ed9d93`, run 40741930571, labelled cold\n\n| job | hosted wall clock | result |\n|---|---:|---|\n| lint | 1,253 s | passed |\n| test-linux | 1,837 s | timeout/cancelled, including cleanup; full workload not completed |\n| test-windows | 1,844 s | timeout/cancelled, including cleanup; full workload not completed |\n| test-macos | no executed duration yet | still queued |\n\nCold is the manager's requested label until the main-push warmer runs with the fixed key. Lint's Rust cache explicitly missed; its key's compiler inventory now contains only the pinned 1.97.1 compiler, not the runner's floating version. Linux and Windows also missed Rust caches. Lint: Bootstrap 65 s, Nx checks 724 s, judged lint 385 s. Windows: Bootstrap 127 s, check step 1,599 s before cancellation, Rust-cache post-step 30 s. Linux: Bootstrap 38 s, check step 1,734 s, cache post-step 19 s. These censored runs do not establish completed suite durations or the 1,620 s bar.\n\nActual execution versus waits: Linux's GitHub main live binary took 908.24 s; its phase timers attribute 792.5 s/84 requests to ChangeToken. The asset binary's 930.55 s includes 911.0 s admission wait; AssetTask, AssetDocument and Cleanup together took about 18.5 s. Those overlapping binary spans must not be added as work. Windows' workflow subprocesses reported 6.95 s, 2.79 s and 219.41 s; its large e2e binaries had not completed at timeout, so this attempt supplies no completed per-binary cost for them. The Linux CD build's reported 365 s includes Cargo build-directory lock wait, not isolated compiler CPU.\n\nThe completed Linux log exposes two further blockers. Its CD and distribution journeys race over maturin's shared native output: rename of target/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/release/onetaskgraph to target/maturin/onetaskgraph fails ENOENT; the dependent CD cases fail afterward. The manager has been asked to choose/approve coordination of those wheel builds versus target ordering; no fix is yet claimed. The required Linear asset journey is refused by Linear's team maximum of 1,000 documents at linear_assets.rs:1194. This quota was escalated immediately; no Linear assertion was weakened and no remote cleanup was performed by this dispatch. This is distinct from the earlier Markdown-rewrite failure already routed to `onetaskgraph-linear-markdown-rewrite`.\n\nThe three consecutive all-project runs with all four jobs at or below 1,620 seconds remain **unproven**. No packaging-profile change, split refactor, larger runner, timing exception, or optional live lane has been applied.\n\n---\n\n## Accounting experiment after `24ed9d93` — reverted, no retained cut\n\nAn unchanged production control and a bounded known-document/numeric-bindings memo both ran the complete cache-budget grid with identical added raw-versus-accounting count assertions. Test execution was 115.674 s control and 116.067 s candidate; complete telemetry was byte-for-byte identical. Whole commands were 173.38 s and 130.02 s respectively, but compilation was 57.22 s versus 13.53 s, so the shorter whole command is **not** evidence of a runtime saving. The candidate passed five node-count tests and 17 accounting/refusal/retry/session tests. A deliberately incorrect memo key was observed failing at the behavioral assertion (first=100 reused 151 instead of 10,150); the earlier mutation stopped at compilation and is not counted as that proof. No pacing change was made and no pacing verification is claimed for this discarded experiment.\n\nThe memo, added experimental tests and workload assertions were all reverted. No production request-accounting optimization is retained; the committed tree remains `24ed9d93`. No case or workload was removed or sampled. The positive committed cut remains exclusion of the unused floating compiler from cache inputs; its hosted saving is still being measured.\n\n---\n\n## Merge validation at `24ed9d93` (2026-10-28)\n\nThe cache-key repair `deca417c` is retained. `24ed9d93` merges main `057b6c30`, preserving its per-platform release packaging and this branch's targeted relative-interpreter pass. The pass now drives the real `release_cd.py package` command with an existing native binary; production release compression stays unchanged. Tampered fixtures keep the approved level-1 compression. The isolated SDK-install proof remains, and main's supported-carrier inventory check is retained.\n\nThe workflow project's `test` target declares the combined graph and real CD-journey read sets, including sources the release recipes build and package. `ci-contexts` keeps its exact graph-only inputs; `ci-jobs` keeps its `.github/`-only inputs. The reconciliation guard was observed failing with a CD read input omitted, then passed with all inputs restored. Nx format-check/lint/typecheck and the graph guards passed before commit.\n\nValidation: the real full distribution journey and relative-interpreter call-site comparison passed after the final fixture restoration. The missed-call-site/unwritable-recorder guard passed before that restoration (which changes only tampered archive compression). Judged lint on the exact final staged tree passed: 39 rules, zero findings, history `20261028T180204Z-beb43`. The clean committed scratch candidate's CD module passed all 12 journeys (736.43 s locally; publication case 548.52 s, including fresh registry-verification builds). That candidate preceded only the tar-fixture compression restoration, which this CD module does not execute; the final full distribution comparison exercises that restoration. Initial failures in the dirty merge tree and in an incompletely bootstrapped scratch checkout were setup failures, not accepted passing evidence. The independent clean-main control at `057b6c30` also passed all 12 journeys (855.90 s locally, publication case 547.22 s). Both controls use frozen dependencies and clean committed trees; no publish assertion changed.\n\nSuppression accounting for this integration: `.github/project.json` rewrites its file-scoped `projects_stay_in_one_js_workspace,external_service_suite_stays_out_of_the_affected_tier` reason: private tooling retains isolated dependencies; actual publication checks use public registry reads but send writes only to loopback, and all real build inputs stay declared. `scripts/test-release-products.py` changes the tar-write line under main's existing `changed_behavior_has_e2e` directive: the malformed-manifest refusal and recovery are exercised by the native distribution journey. No assertion was relaxed. The `Tallies` suppression from `deca417c` is described below.\n\nNo hosted CI run of `24ed9d93` has completed; [run 40741930571](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40741930571) is queued. The preceding run’s macOS job ended cancelled at 18:06:00Z without a runner or steps; its queue interval is not execution time. The three-consecutive all-project runs at or below 1,620 seconds remain unproven. No timing exception, cheaper packaging profile, or split refactor has been applied. Linear's live Markdown failure remains routed to `onetaskgraph-linear-markdown-rewrite`, outside this branch.\n\n---\n\n## Current state at `deca417c` (2026-10-28)\n\n`deca417c` removes only the runner's unused floating `stable` toolchain before Rust-cache setup in the four new PR jobs and their warmers. All builds retain the compiler pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`. The cache action hashes every installed compiler, so this removes an input that did not produce the cached builds. Hosted saving is not yet measured; new attempts remain labelled cold with respect to the main warmer until it has run with this setup.\n\nValidation before this commit: `github-workflows` format-check, lint, typecheck and test all passed through Nx; the workflow guard was first observed failing, then passed for removed, reordered and disabled toolchain-cleanup steps. `cargo fmt -p onetaskgraph-e2e-support -- --check` and `git diff --check` passed. Judged lint passed with 39 rules passed and zero findings (history `20261028T171814Z-b9937`). One narrow suppression was added at `crates/onetaskgraph-e2e-support/src/fixtures.rs`, on `Tallies`: it is private response-rendering state of the shared loopback board, whose placement is already fixed by the shared harness layout; moving the helper alone would expose private board state across a crate boundary. No test case, timeout or coverage floor changed.\n\nThe manager approved publishing now (reply 123), without waiting for superseded macOS: run 40721997875's macOS job was still queued at the last read. No cancellation request was sent. The workflow may supersede it through its own concurrency rule. The manager also approved production request-accounting parsing work, conditional on unchanged counts/refusals/pacing and a comparison against raw counts over the cache-budget workload (replies 118–120); that experiment has not yet landed.\n\nThe targeted relative-interpreter pass is committed and validated. The 1,620-second / three-consecutive-hosted-run criterion is **not yet established**. No cheaper packaging profile or overrun exception has been applied.\n\nThis dispatch validated `65e0a5ca`, then repaired a recording failure found by judged lint in `c8c10f66`: an unwritable call-site record now refuses the interpreter call with the failed operation and a repair action. The old recorder was observed returning zero with a directory at its record path. The regression test now forces that real error, in addition to accepting the complete targeted pass and rejecting a deliberately omitted workspace-check caller.\n\nChecks on the final `c8c10f66` tree, run immediately before committing (the commit itself changed no content):\n- `bash scripts/check-python-call-sites-enforced.sh`: pass, including the deliberately missed caller and unwritable record.\n- `bash scripts/check-relative-interpreter.sh scripts/test-distribution.sh`: pass, running the full distribution journey and comparing its recorded callers against the targeted pass.\n- `bash scripts/check-bash4-array-builtins.sh`, `bash scripts/check-tar-drained.sh`, `bash -n` for the two repaired scripts, and `git diff --check`: pass.\n- `just lint-llm-diff origin/main`: pass, 39 rules passed, zero findings (history `20261028T160203Z-6ed60`). No suppression was added or changed by `c8c10f66`.\n- Before the recorder repair, `bash scripts/check-ci-recipes.sh`, the default `bash scripts/check-relative-interpreter.sh`, `bash scripts/check-workspace-config.sh`, and `bun run biome format scripts/project.json` passed on `65e0a5ca`; the repair changes neither recipe selection nor project formatting/configuration.\n\nThe targeted pass alone (`bash scripts/check-relative-interpreter.sh scripts/test-python-call-sites.sh`) measured **13.21 s wall / 10.91 s user / 2.94 s system** on this host with its existing binary/cache, on the final tree before commit. This is not a hosted saving measurement.\n\nHosted evidence for the prior `c8c10f66` head: [run 40721997875](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40721997875), first attempt, **does not meet the timing criterion**:\n\n| job | hosted duration | result |\n|---|---:|---|\n| lint | 1,406 s | success (Nx checks 876 s, judged lint 430 s) |\n| test-windows | 1,817 s | cancelled at timeout |\n| test-linux | 1,884 s | cancelled at timeout, including cancellation cleanup |\n| test-macos | no executed duration | cancelled while queued at 18:06:00Z; no runner and no steps |\n\nWindows Bootstrap took 41 s, Linux 8 s, lint 30 s. Windows `scripts:test` never started before timeout, so this attempt does not establish the targeted interpreter pass's hosted saving. Windows task-output spans (overlapping, not additive): github-workflows 1,534 s; TypeScript SDK 1,267 s; Linear e2e 1,168 s; distribution-check 1,108 s; GitHub Projects e2e 1,098 s; janitor 1,042 s; distribution-test 931 s; main e2e 730 s. These are first-to-last combined-target output spans, not isolated CPU costs. In particular, github-workflows includes a long gap between tests and budgets: its actual test subprocess durations were 3.49 s (`ci-jobs`), 2.14 s (`ci-contexts`) and 124.82 s (main suite), about 130.5 s total; the 1,534 s span must not be read as suite runtime.\n\n### Linux comparison with the last completed attempt\n\n[Run 40665510604](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40665510604), `2b3f85c4`, passed Linux in 1,496 s. It restored a 790 MB Rust cache (`v0-rust-test-linux-Linux-x64-7a4f4005-1c8bf073`). The `c8c10f66` attempt found **no Rust cache**, under `...5e270797-ab2dafca`. Its cache inputs included an unused runner compiler changing from 1.99.0 to 1.99.1; the pinned build compiler stayed 1.97.1. Between these heads no Rust source, Cargo manifest or Cargo.lock changed—only the targeted interpreter pass and recorder repair. There is no intervening main merge.\n\nThe live-janitor journey itself was 25.38 s before and 25.72 s after, but began much later (about 5m45s vs 18m after job start). Core doctests likewise moved from about 3m50s to 12m30s after job start. The earlier instrumented SDK phase completed at 09:57:16; the new one had not completed at timeout. The cold dependency build is therefore a material cause of the Linux regression; these runs do not isolate any additional runner-draw effect. This is **not** evidence that product cases were added or that the interpreter optimization added Rust compilation. The forthcoming cache configuration removes only the unused floating compiler from producer/consumer cache inputs; its hosted saving is not yet measured.\n\nLinux also reported a separate failure in `real_linear_applies_every_declared_capability_and_leaves_no_residue`: Linear rewrote document Markdown (including links and escaped brackets/comment terminator) differently from its existing ruling. The manager routed this to the separate `onetaskgraph-linear-markdown-rewrite` node (reply 116); this branch leaves it untouched and weakens no assertion.\n\nThe manager directed that the three-run sequence wait until a cut or ruling makes it reachable. The cheaper packaging profile and overrun exception remain pending. Additional fixture experiments are measurements only, all reverted so far: parsed-document reuse 142.8 s vs 145.0 s baseline; point-price reuse 154.8 s; proxy JSON decode-once 165.8 s. Every experiment passed the full workload and produced identical telemetry; no useful saving is established. A second original-code control passed in 164.96 s, versus 165.76 s for proxy decode-once under the later host load, confirming that experiment establishes no saving. All prototype changes and diagnostic instrumentation were reverted before `deca417c`. The manager subsequently approved production request-accounting optimization (replies 118–120); no production optimization has landed yet. Diagnostic setup/sequence timing was also removed: at 10x, setup ~21 s, task sequences 9–20 s, projects 32–34 s, assets ~56 s, documents/manifests 111–114 s. No test case or workload was removed or sampled.\n\nRun [40720154649](https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40720154649), on the earlier `65e0a5ca`, completed cancelled after the new head arrived (no cancellation was sent by this dispatch). Its `deny` passed, so the previous head's advisory failure did not recur there.\n\nThe following report is historical evidence from earlier commits. Its duration projections and claims of current state are superseded by this section wherever they differ.\n\n---\n\n## State at head `2b3f85c4`: the 1,620 s bar is NOT met. The decisions that could close it are with the user.\n\nEvery job keeps `timeout-minutes: 30` and every case is kept. The manager has put the remaining trade-offs to the user (below), and none is applied.\n\n### Hosted durations, latest run of the finished head\n\nRun https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40665510604, head `2b3f85c4`. All three test jobs restored a full-match Rust cache.\n\n| job | hosted | outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| `lint` | 654 s | failure: `workspace:deny`, on a RustSec advisory published today (below) |\n| `test-linux` | 1,496 s | success, credentialed live lanes included |\n| `test-windows` | 1,819 s | cancelled at the 30-minute limit |\n| `test-macos` | 1,827 s | cancelled at the 30-minute limit |\n\n`lint` and the required `deny` both fail on **RUSTSEC-2026-0414** (`pulldown-cmark`, \"Exponential event growth with `ENABLE_WIKILINKS`\"). It is an advisory against an existing dependency, not something this branch changed. It refuses every pull request until the crate is bumped, and that is raised with the manager. The judged lint over this branch (`just lint-llm-diff origin/main`) passes on `2b3f85c4`: 37 rules passed, 0 failed.\n\n### Why the time is out of reach: the runners are saturated\n\nTwo diagnostic runs on this branch carried a CPU sampler and a 24-minute step limit, since reverted (`2b3f85c4`). They are diagnostics, not evidence for the finished head.\n- Run https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40643397175 (`9a2eb30f`) and run https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/40656765389 (`812d9a4f`).\n- Both restored a warm, full-match cache.\n- Mean CPU busy over the test step: **Windows 97%, macOS 99%, Linux 99%**.\n- test-windows and test-macos ran past the 24-minute step limit on both runs; test-linux did on 40656765389.\n\nWith a 4-core runner saturated, the job time is its total CPU work ÷ 4. Only cuts that remove CPU work move it; running tests concurrently or reordering them does not.\n\n### Cost breakdown\n\n**Hosted, per phase and suite** (first to last output line of each task; tasks overlap):\n\n| task | macOS fast (117823918287, 708 s job) | macOS slow (117877995105, 1,731 s) | Windows faster (117877995195, 1,446 s) | Windows slow (117898071094, cancelled 1,815 s) | Windows warm, current tree (122709563754, past the 24-minute step) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `onetaskgraph-github-projects-e2e` | 333 s | 1,016 s | 917 s | 1,208 s | 983 s |\n| `onetaskgraph-linear-e2e` | 251 s | 828 s | 885 s | 1,162 s | 857 s |\n| `onetaskgraph-live-janitor` | 270 s | 919 s | 1,008 s | 1,260 s | 1,168 s |\n| `sdk-typescript` | 246 s | 970 s | 827 s | 1,222 s | — |\n| `scripts:distribution-test` | 222 s | 845 s | 840 s | 1,230 s | 1,002 s |\n| `scripts:distribution-check` | — | — | 893 s | 1,303 s | — |\n| `onetaskgraph-e2e` | 34 s | 103 s | 330 s | 454 s | 439 s |\n| `scripts:test` (job tail) | 22 s | 36 s | 265 s | — | — |\n\n- The first four columns are runs from before the merge of `main`; the first three ran on `78290f71` and `43dac669`, the slow Windows draw on `43dac669`.\n- **Slow draws ran 2–2.6× the fast ones on macOS** and ~1.25× on Windows, with the same image and the same cache.\n- The release build inside `distribution-test` alone took 300 s on Windows and 780 s on the slow macOS draw.\n\n**CPU per task, measured alone on this host** (user+sys CPU-s, at `60464a1c` plus this round's fixes; about 6,400 in total):\n\n| rank | task | CPU-s | note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `onetaskgraph-github-projects-e2e` | 1,507 | its `cache_budget` test alone ~850: the binaries it spawns ~525, its loopback board ~309 |\n| 2 | `scripts:test` | 1,260 | `check-relative-interpreter.sh` (the second distribution journey) 281; `check-ci-recipes.sh` 245 |\n| 3 | `scripts:distribution-test` | 709 | the release build ~335; `test-release-products.py` 206 s wall, of which 37 verifier refusal cases are 131 s |\n| 4 | `onetaskgraph-e2e` | 631 | |\n| 5 | workspace compile | ~620 | cleaned, `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0` |\n| 6 | `onetaskgraph-linear-e2e` | 420 | its `cache_budget` test is 222 s wall |\n| 7 | `scripts:distribution-check` | 232 | 368 s wall here; 893–1,303 s on hosted Windows, where `check-release-pr-sync.sh` alone is 827 s |\n\n### Cuts this round, every case kept\n\n- **`live_pair` no longer runs a mutated test binary** (`495fd8c9`, with `812d9a4f`).\n  - The problem: `check-fixture-discrimination.sh` shares the build directory, so its scratch copy left its deliberately mutated `plugin` and `live` executables beside the real ones. `live_pair` picks the newest executable whose dep-info names those sources, so after the check had run (beside the suites, as on macOS and Windows), **11 of its 14 tests failed**, each after a 30 s wait.\n  - The fix: the copy now builds from a directory of another name, and the check refuses a run that wrote no dep-info naming the copy, or any naming the real crate.\n  - Measured: `live_pair` takes 12–16 s for 14 tests, against 37–40 s and failing before.\n- **Janitor offline journey** (`14d1f9b0`). Each run's loopback shutdown waited out `serve_forever`'s half-second poll. Polling every 20 ms takes the 36 cases from 101 s to 23 s on this host. CPU is unchanged (it was wait), so the hosted saving is wall time on an occupied slot, not CPU.\n- **`scripts/ci-job.sh` waits for its phases by process id** (`824d0679`), so a background helper can never hold a job.\n\n### Measured and not landed\n\n| option | measurement | outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| Workspace crates at `opt-level = 1` | tests −15% (`onetaskgraph-e2e`) and −28% (`github-projects-e2e`) CPU; but a cleaned, non-incremental workspace compile goes from 615–639 to 2,590 CPU-s | net loss; dropped |\n| Only `onetaskgraph-e2e-support` at `opt-level = 1` | the GitHub `cache_budget` test 852 → 782 CPU-s; that crate's compile 16 → 31 s wall | ~2–3% of a job; drafted as a follow-up |\n| Unifying dependency features across the per-crate builds | 161 distinct workspace units, but the compile CPU is the same as one unified build (~630 vs 615–639) | no saving; dropped |\n| Shared setup for the two distribution passes | about 25 s of a 337 s relative pass | not built, by the manager's ruling |\n| Concurrency inside the Linear `cache_budget` test, or the verifier refusal cases | the runners are saturated, so there is no idle CPU to use | not built |\n\n### Pending decisions (the user's, via the manager)\n\n1. **Option A or B for the duplicated distribution journey** on macOS and Windows (one run instead of two): ~280 CPU-s and the `scripts:test` tail. Both runs stay until then (option C).\n2. **Package a cheaper profile in the pull-request distribution journey**, with the release workflow unchanged: ~335 CPU-s locally, and 300–780 s of hosted wall clock.\n3. **Whether a measured overrun is accepted** if the bar stays out of reach after those.\n\nNo larger runner, no split of a job's work, and no case removed or sampled. Those are ruled out or need the user.\n\n## Earlier evidence (previous dispatches, labelled with the commits they were taken on)\n\n### Local wall clock on this host (taken on `9048d196`)\n\nHow these were taken:\n- **Commands:** `just bootstrap`, then `just ci-<job>`, under `CI=true`. Credentials and every `ONETASKGRAPH_*`, `GH_*`, `LINEAR_*` and `NX_*` variable were cleared.\n- **Where:** a measurement worktree whose cargo target holds the dependency builds the warmer leaves. Every workspace crate was cleaned before each \"first\" run.\n- **\"First\":** an empty Nx cache *and* an empty Nx workspace DB. Nx 23 shares the task DB across git worktrees unless `NX_WORKSPACE_DATA_DIRECTORY` is set; it is set per run.\n- **\"Restored\":** the same Nx cache and DB, kept from the first run.\n- **Workloads:**\n  - *Lockfile*: a commit bumping one dependency (`fancy-regex` 0.19.0 → 0.19.2) in `Cargo.lock` alone, which selects all 22 projects the lockfile reaches.\n  - *One-project*: a one-line change to `onetaskgraph-linear`, which selects 9 projects. The spike document was not readable from this host, so this choice comes from main's first-parent history: `onetaskgraph-linear` was the commonest single-project merge, 3 of 14, from 2026-09-15 to 10-15.\n- **Host load:** this is a shared 20-core host, and the 1-minute load average is given for each run.\n- **Commit:** taken on `9048d196` plus the workload commit. The later commits do not change which tasks a job selects. `5abacd3f` puts test-windows's phases in sequence, and that figure is derived below from Nx's per-task record. `c811aeb8` adds two sub-second checks to `scripts:test`.\n\n| job | lockfile, first | lockfile, restored | one-project, first | one-project, restored |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `lint` (`ci-lint`; bootstrap 8–24 s) | 818 s (load 78→52) | 156 s | 1,021 s (load 42) | 104 s |\n| `test-linux` | 2,841 s (load 35→46) | 2,405 s (load 46→62) | 2,247 s (load 61→48) | 1,243 s (load 20) |\n| `test-macos` | 942 s (load 5→9) | 653 s | 747 s (load 14) | 622 s |\n| `test-windows` | same commands as `test-macos` (phases side by side) | | | |\n\nNotes on the table:\n- **test-windows and test-macos select the identical tasks on both workloads** (`ci-job.sh --select-only`, compared), and now run the same phases side by side, so test-macos's figures stand for both. For reference, the three phases summed in sequence would take 1,773 / 1,360 / 1,598 / 794 s.\n- **test-linux restored is barely faster than first by design.** Coverage is never served from cache (AGENTS.md), and coverage now runs the non-live tests.\n- **Excluded from the lint figures:**\n  - The judged llmlint step: hosted 239–480 s per the spike, and 94 s locally for this diff.\n  - The visual-docs capture.\n  - The release-PR-only pool-maintenance budget: 836 s locally, under load 78.\n- **The warmer's own build cost on this host, cold:**\n  - The plain legs (macOS and Windows) took 762 s, of which the release build was 3 m 42 s.\n  - The Linux leg's instrumented build added 478 s.\n  - With an empty cargo target, test-macos's commands took 1,264 s, against 942 s with the warmer's dependencies.\n\n### Projected hosted durations (projections from `9048d196`, superseded by the saturated-runner measurements above)\n\nHow the projections are built:\n- **Setup** is this branch's hosted setup before the recipe step: Linux about 40 s, Windows about 110 s, macOS about 70 s.\n- **Phases** are added from the spike's hosted per-phase figures (median / p90) of today's `check (<os>)` jobs, which restore main's warm cache.\n- **Cross-check:** the local cold-to-warm ratio applied to hosted cold runs.\n\n| job | median | p90 | basis |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `lint` | ~800 s | ~870 s | measured: 575–864 s on 13 successful hosted runs |\n| `test-linux` | ~1,250 s | ~1,650 s | measured passes of 1,219–1,533 s, with partially warm caches |\n| `test-macos` | ~1,190 s | ~1,530 s | setup 70 + test 540/824 + distribution-check 114/128 + distribution-test 471/512, summed. That is an upper bound, because the phases run side by side. Cross-check: the local warm/cold ratio of 942/1,264 applied to the hosted cold run's ~1,830 s gives ~1,370 s. |\n| `test-windows` | ~1,750 s | ~2,240 s | the local warm/cold ratio (942/1,264 = 0.745) applied to the four completed cold hosted runs, side by side: 2,342 s, 2,836 s, 2,955 s and 3,004 s. The lower bound, from the spike's warm per-phase figures with the phases side by side, is max(test 778/1,189, distribution 660/760) + setup 110, so ~890 / ~1,300 s. |\n\nNotes on the projections:\n- **test-linux:** no successful hosted run has had a warm cache. The 1,650 s p90 is a projection, assuming today's `check (ubuntu-latest)` no longer runs a live session beside it, which ends when `onetaskgraph-ci-budgets` removes that job.\n- **The projected longest of the four is test-windows**, the only one projected over 1,800 s.\n\n**test-windows, the manager's decision, and what was done.** I asked the manager to accept the ~250 s overrun projected while the phases ran in sequence. The manager did not accept it: accepting a Windows overrun is the user's decision, and their standing ruling is to bring Windows down without removing a case, so the manager is taking these figures to the user. On the manager's direction, the side-by-side phases were made to hold, with two separate fixes:\n\n1. **Windows ephemeral-port exhaustion, the cause of the first trial's loopback failures** (run 39087511575, `9048d196`).\n   - A fixture's own `TcpStream::connect` panicked with OS error 10055, WSAENOBUFS (\"the system lacked sufficient buffer space or because a queue was full\"), and every client in the same seconds could not reach its server.\n   - Every loopback fixture answers `Connection: close`, so each request is a new TCP connection. Counted with `strace` on Linux, the GitHub cache-budget grid opens **34,714** loopback connections and the Linear one **10,440**.\n   - Windows' default dynamic port range is **16,384** ports, which runs out while closed connections wait out TIME_WAIT.\n   - Fix (`a0c29af0`): test-windows widens the range before its tests (`netsh int ipv4 set dynamicport tcp start=10000 num=55535`), and `ci-jobs.spec.mjs` refuses a test-windows job without it, or with it after the tests. No case changed.\n2. **The live-janitor journey's hang guard** (`00b83b6a`). The flake on `6ebab9d1` was its 30 s wall-clock subprocess timeout, which expired on the 24,300-issue workload beside the release build. The janitor runs on a virtual clock, so the limit was only ever a hang guard. It is now 300 s, with every case and assertion kept.\n\nAlso, transport errors now name their OS cause (`8821e127`).\n\n**The trial.** A run counts only if every phase and suite of test-windows finished inside the job with no failure, and only after the fixes.\n- Runs that did not count:\n  - 39116770335 and 39121049682, cancelled at their 30-minute limit before every suite had finished.\n  - 39123274311, whose two attempts were complete but came before the port fix.\n  - 39132645011, which hit its 60-minute limit with no log kept.\n- The counted runs used the manager's temporary diagnostics, both reverted in `ce7792b6`: `timeout-minutes: 60` on the job (`ebec6bbb`) and a 50-minute step limit to keep any log (`2db095b4`). Two consecutive runs completed every phase with no failure, cold-built, on https://github.com/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/actions/runs/39138640022:\n\n| run | test, budgets | distribution-check | distribution-test (cold release build) | job |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| attempt 1 | 2,828 s | 1,241 s | 2,740 s (14 m 35 s) | **2,955 s** |\n| attempt 2 (re-run) | 2,876 s | 1,061 s | 2,801 s (15 m 09 s) | **3,004 s** |\n\n- **The final tree keeps `timeout-minutes: 30` and no diagnostic.** It is identical to `a0c29af0`.\n- **What is left:**\n  - Measured: every completed cold run exceeds 1,800 s (2,342, 2,836, 2,955 and 3,004 s).\n  - Projected warm (an estimate): the local warm/cold ratio of 0.745 applied to these runs gives ~1,750–2,240 s. The spike-based lower bound is ~1,300 s at p90.\n  - The manager first ruled out raising Nx's parallelism (C). After two consecutive hosted runs with no flake, the manager approved four test slots on Windows (`28a12c48`). The run on that head (39165503331, warm) was still cancelled at the limit. Cut B has nothing to cut in CI (below).\n  - **test-windows is therefore over 1,800 s warm as well as cold:** 2,138 s measured warm with three slots, and cancelled at 1,816 s warm with four. Whether to accept that overrun on all-affected pull requests is the user's decision, which the manager is taking to them.\n  - The remaining options, for the user's decision: accept the overrun on all-affected pull requests (the one-project figure is well inside the limit), or measure the warm job once the warmer has run on main.\n\n**The four leads the amendment names, for test-windows:**\n- **github-workflows suite subprocess cost.** The policy tests were split into three files that `node --test` runs at once (`ba8aa723`). Hosted Windows `github-workflows:test` measured 448 s before the split and 439–502 s after it, so no measurable saving. On a 4-core runner beside the cargo builds, the subprocesses are CPU-bound either way.\n- **Build-cache reuse for the github-projects e2e build.** This is what the warmer provides: the dependency units are restored from main. The saving is the dependency share of the cold builds, about 25% of the job locally (1,264 → 942 s), which is projection-only until the warmer has run on main.\n- **Python SDK bootstrap.** The CLI copy is left out of the SDK's environment in CI (`38c61b1a`). Windows setup went from about 6.5 min to about 1.5 min, measured hosted. `sdk-python:bootstrap` had been about 270 s, and the whole Bootstrap step is now 35 s.\n- **Per-case virtual-environment installs in the packaging test.** There is nothing to cut in CI, so this saves nothing:\n  - `verify-release-products.py` creates a venv only under `--install`.\n  - Every `--install` case in `check-release-packaging.sh` is a refusal that exits before creating one.\n  - The SDK artifact test creates a single venv, not one per case.\n\n## Where each old job and check runs now\n\n- **`check (ubuntu-latest)`:**\n  - format-check, lint, typecheck and generate-check → `lint`, step \"Format, lint and type-check…\" (`ci-lint`).\n  - test (live lanes included), coverage, distribution-check and distribution-test → `test-linux` (`ci-test-linux`).\n- **`check (windows-latest)`** → `test-windows` (`ci-test-windows`): every suite once, with budgets, distribution-check and distribution-test. Its lint phases → `lint`.\n- **`macOS check and install proofs` (`macos`)** → `test-macos` (`ci-test-macos`). The macOS-lane selection is folded into the job's own condition: it is skipped on same-repository `release-plz-` branches. The `check (macos-latest)` and `install path (macos-latest)` carriers read test-macos.\n- **`install path (ubuntu-latest)` / `(windows-latest)`** → dropped as a cut. Each ran the same `just distribution-test` the test jobs run: test-linux runs it once under the relative-interpreter PATH, and test-windows and test-macos run it plainly.\n- **`deny`** → `lint`, `workspace:deny` inside `ci-lint`.\n- **`pr-title`** → `lint`, step \"Check the pull request title\".\n- **`llmlint`** → `lint`, steps \"Validate the llmlint config\" and \"Lint the changed files with llmlint\".\n- **`notignored.yml` `suppressions`** → `lint`, step \"Comment the suppressions this change adds\".\n- **`visual-docs.yml` capture and report for `x86_64`** → `lint`, through screencomp's composite actions (`screencomp`, `screencomp/visual-docs`, `visual-docs-pages`, `visual-docs-aggregate`) as steps.\n- **`macos-lanes`** → no counterpart needed: each job selects for itself.\n- **The release-PR shortcut** → `.github/release-shortcut.mjs`. test-linux, test-windows and test-macos run it, and `lint` never consults it. A head whose tree is a tree main passed, plus only release-plz's version and changelog edits, skips the tests. `.github/tests/release-shortcut.test.mjs` drives it over a scratch repository.\n\n## Cuts applied, and the time each saved\n\n- **The `install path` legs dropped:** 2,636 runner-s per PR run, per the spike.\n- **Bootstrap toolchain cached,** keyed on the pinned tool versions and the OS. Hosted Bootstrap is now 7–35 s, against 510 / 907 / 672 s median on Ubuntu / Windows / macOS.\n- **Non-live tests run once.** On Linux they run under coverage, serving both pass/fail and the floor, and each live crate's `test` runs only its credentialed journey; a gate fails if a binary is run by neither. On macOS and Windows they run once, uninstrumented (Linux-only coverage, approved). This removes the second test run, up to the 555 s local Nx-cold test span.\n- **The `live_pair` waits are gone.** All 14 `live_pair` tests finish in 12–16 s on this host at `2b3f85c4`, where six of them took about 22 s each in the spike. Between those two points they were failing for 30 s each against the fixture-discrimination check's mutated binary, fixed in `495fd8c9`.\n- **The `cache_budget` grid measures its cells at once** (`a7976ccf`). It took 214 s on this host at `c811aeb8`, at load 12–15. The spike's pre-change figure is only a lower bound, since that run was killed at 98 s, so this cut's saving is **not established**.\n- **The ChangeToken checks are batched** so that one settle wait covers a batch, with each change kind asserted against its own before and after token.\n- **The asset journey and the loopback e2e binaries run beside the main live session.**\n- **The release journey's tampered archive fixtures are written at zlib level 1,** and the SDK's CLI copy is not built in CI.\n- **The Rust cache warmer on main:** the cold release build alone is 13–17 min on Windows and macOS hosted.\n\nNo test, platform, live or e2e case was removed or sampled beyond the approved trades above. No suite is split across jobs, and every live and loopback case runs on every pull request inside a required job.\n\n## Tests\n\n- **The cache-budget concurrency race is fixed** (`6a56296a`). Before the fix, the two concurrent passes of `cache_budget.rs` both attached to the simulated clock as client 0, and the second attach was refused once the reattach grace ran out: macOS runs 39130029053 (5 s grace) and 39136328216 (30 s grace). Now the clock is started for two clients, and the second pass runs as client 1 through a listener of its own that shares the board's ledger. Client 1 attaches once during setup, and setup asserts that its process reaches its own listener; I watched that assertion fail with client 1 pointed at client 0's listener. Every case and assertion stays. The whole test passed locally at 482 s, under a host load of about 40 on 20 cores.\n\n- **`.github/tests/ci-jobs.spec.mjs`** (Nx project `github-workflows`, rooted at `.github/`, target `ci-jobs`). It reads the workflow files and the resolved run graph, and fails when:\n  - one of the four jobs is missing, renamed, given `needs`, or given a timeout other than 30;\n  - the visual docs capture leaves `lint` or runs through the reusable workflow;\n  - a job behind one of today's required contexts goes missing;\n  - a cache key omits the lockfile or the pinned tool version it depends on;\n  - the warmer parts from the key a test job restores.\n- **`.github/tests/policy-routing.test.mjs`** runs the warmer's real `release-merge` step over real revisions. It proves that a release merge schedules no Mac job, that an ordinary push schedules `warm-macos`, and that the lifecycle counts 3.\n- **`scripts/check-ci-recipes.sh`** (`scripts:test`) drives each recipe over a scratch copy, with a committed one-project change and with a change to nothing it tests. It asserts from the recipe's own output which targets ran, and that a failing target makes the recipe exit non-zero.\n- **`scripts/check-warm-rust-cache.sh`** and **`scripts/check-sync-python-sdk.sh`** (`scripts:test`, new) hold the warmer's builds and the SDK sync's two modes.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n","created_at":"2026-10-17T03:28:49Z","updated_at":"2026-10-31T12:55:45Z","closed_at":"2026-10-31T12:55:41Z","merged_at":"2026-10-31T12:55:41Z","merge_commit_sha":"61ba820e16a646c4c41d41e8c4aa5265eeb9b8a3","assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"labels":[],"milestone":null,"draft":false,"commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/pulls/3642/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/pulls/3642/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/issues/3642/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nickderobertis/onetaskgraph/statuses/911aba509243f5d8fce6b49ecafdf6d9aa2d1938","head":{"label":"nickderobertis:nick/3632/onetaskgraph-ci-speedup","